# Cargo's build output. Shared by the whole workspace, so one entry covers it.
/target

# Old rustfmt backups.
**/*.rs.bk

# Editors and OS junk.
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# NOTE: Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored.
# It is committed for binaries and libraries alike -- see the README section
# "Commit Cargo.lock". If you copy this file into a library and add an ignore
# rule for Cargo.lock, you are giving up reproducible CI for nothing.

# Local environment values -- see the README section "Environment variables".
# `.env.example` is the committed template and has to survive the rule above it,
# which is what the negation is for.
#
# Two things about that negation. It works only because these patterns match
# files: git will not re-include a path inside an excluded *directory*, so
# `secrets/` followed by `!secrets/example.toml` silently does nothing. And
# neither rule affects a file that is already tracked -- if a `.env` has ever
# been committed, `git rm --cached .env` and rotate whatever was in it, because
# it is still in the history either way.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
